The table structure information is ({database name: {schema name: [table name]}}): 
{'bigquery-public-data': {'san_francisco': ['film_locations', 'bikeshare_stations', 'street_trees', 'sfpd_incidents', 'bikeshare_status', 'bikeshare_trips', '311_service_requests', 'sffd_service_calls']}}
Begin Exploring Related Columns
Relevant columns and their potential usage for computing “total distance traveled by each bike”  
(from tables bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_trips and …bikeshare_stations):

bikeshare_trips  
• bike_number : primary key we will aggregate on (one row = one trip for that bike)  
• start_station_id / end_station_id : link every trip to its geographic coordinates in the station table  
• start_station_name / end_station_name : optional labels for checks or reports  
• duration_sec / start_date : optional filters (e.g., remove obviously bad records)

bikeshare_stations  
• station_id : joins to start_station_id / end_station_id  
• latitude / longitude : coordinates needed to build GEOGRAPHY points and measure great‑circle distance  
• landmark / installation_date : optional filters (e.g., San Francisco only, station became active after a date)

Typical conditions we may apply  
• WHERE ss.latitude IS NOT NULL AND es.latitude IS NOT NULL       -- ensure we have coordinates  
• WHERE t.duration_sec>0                                         -- ignore corrupted trips  
• WHERE ST_DISTANCE(...)>0                                        -- ignore 0‑length “re‑dock” events  

Below are 10 sample BigQuery SQL statements (in increasing complexity) that explore the data and end with a query that produces the requested per‑bike totals.  
Every statement is self‑contained, uses only the allowed tables, and returns at most 20 rows so they can be run safely while you explore.

```sql
-- 1. Peek at a few trips: bike id, start/end station IDs and dates
SELECT bike_number,
       start_station_id,
       end_station_id,
       start_date,
       end_date
FROM   `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_trips`
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Look at some station definitions with their coordinates
SELECT station_id,
       name,
       latitude,
       longitude
FROM   `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_stations`
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Join one side: attach start‑station latitude/longitude to each trip
SELECT t.trip_id,
       t.bike_number,
       ss.station_id        AS start_station_id,
       ss.latitude          AS start_lat,
       ss.longitude         AS start_lon
FROM   `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_trips`     AS t
JOIN   `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_stations`  AS ss
ON     t.start_station_id = ss.station_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Join both sides: start & end coordinates for the same trip
SELECT t.trip_id,
       t.bike_number,
       ss.latitude AS start_lat, ss.longitude AS start_lon,
       es.latitude AS end_lat,   es.longitude AS end_lon
FROM   `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_trips`     AS t
JOIN   `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_stations`  AS ss
  ON   t.start_station_id = ss.station_id
JOIN   `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_stations`  AS es
  ON   t.end_station_id   = es.station_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Compute great‑circle distance (in meters) for each of those sample trips
SELECT t.trip_id,
       t.bike_number,
       ROUND(
         ST_DISTANCE(
           ST_GEOGPOINT(ss.longitude, ss.latitude),
           ST_GEOGPOINT(es.longitude, es.latitude)
         ), 1) AS distance_m
FROM   `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_trips`     AS t
JOIN   `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_stations`  AS ss
  ON   t.start_station_id = ss.station_id
JOIN   `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_stations`  AS es
  ON   t.end_station_id   = es.station_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Same as #5 but keep only trips that actually moved (>0 m)
SELECT t.trip_id,
       t.bike_number,
       ROUND(
         ST_DISTANCE(
           ST_GEOGPOINT(ss.longitude, ss.latitude),
           ST_GEOGPOINT(es.longitude, es.latitude)
         ), 1) AS distance_m
FROM   `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_trips`     AS t
JOIN   `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_stations`  AS ss
  ON   t.start_station_id = ss.station_id
JOIN   `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_stations`  AS es
  ON   t.end_station_id   = es.station_id
WHERE  ST_DISTANCE(
         ST_GEOGPOINT(ss.longitude, ss.latitude),
         ST_GEOGPOINT(es.longitude, es.latitude)
       ) > 0
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Aggregate: total meters traveled per bike (sample 20 bikes)
SELECT bike_number,
       ROUND(SUM(distance_m)/1000, 2) AS total_km
FROM   (
         SELECT t.bike_number,
                ST_DISTANCE(
                  ST_GEOGPOINT(ss.longitude, ss.latitude),
                  ST_GEOGPOINT(es.longitude, es.latitude)
                ) AS distance_m
         FROM   `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_trips`     AS t
         JOIN   `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_stations`  AS ss
           ON   t.start_station_id = ss.station_id
         JOIN   `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_stations`  AS es
           ON   t.end_station_id   = es.station_id
       )
GROUP  BY bike_number
LIMIT 20;
```

